  triviaValueText: {fileID: 1322834446}
  countryValueText: {fileID: 1868073867}
  rocketImage: {fileID: 1586570099}
  pageIndicatorText: {fileID: 1301670829}
  detailScrollRect: {fileID: 418608122}
  rockets:
  - id: chinese_fire_arrow
    displayName: Chinese Fire Arrow
    correctZoneId: 13TH_CENTURY
    zoneLabel: 13th Century
    country: China
    keyAdvance: The world's first gunpowder-propelled rocket, first used in battle
      at Kai-Keng in 1232 against Mongol invaders.
    triviaFact: The word "rocket" traces back to the Italian rocchetta, meaning "little
      spindle" - a nod to the long, thin tube shape these early fire arrows shared
      with a spinning bobbin.
    spriteName: chinese_fire_arrow
  - id: wan_hu_chair
    displayName: Wan Hu's Rocket Chair
    correctZoneId: 16TH_CENTURY
    zoneLabel: 16th Century (Legend)
    country: China
    keyAdvance: 'A legendary attempt at the world''s first crewed rocket flight:
      a chair fitted with 47 gunpowder rockets and two kites, meant to launch a person
      into the sky.'
    triviaFact: The story says Wan Hu vanished in a cloud of smoke the moment the
      rockets were lit, and was never seen again - no record confirms it actually
      happened, but the tale has been told for centuries as rocketry's first (failed)
      human spaceflight attempt.
    spriteName: wan_hu_chair
  - id: tipu_sultan_rockets
    displayName: Mysorean Rockets
    correctZoneId: LATE_18TH_CENTURY
    zoneLabel: Late 1700s
    country: India
    keyAdvance: The first iron-cased military rockets, giving them more range and
      thrust than the fragile bamboo and paper rockets used elsewhere at the time.
    triviaFact: Fielded by Hyder Ali and his son Tipu Sultan, the Kingdom of Mysore's
      rocket corps numbered about 5,000 soldiers - roughly a seventh of the entire
      army - and their battlefield success against British forces directly inspired
      Britain to develop its own military rockets.
    spriteName: tipu_sultan_rockets
  - id: congreve_rocket
    displayName: Congreve Rocket
    correctZoneId: 1805
    zoneLabel: 1805
    country: United Kingdom
    keyAdvance: The first standardized, mass-produced military rocket system in the
      West, developed by Colonel William Congreve after studying captured Indian
      rockets.
    triviaFact: Congreve rockets bombarding Fort McHenry in the War of 1812 inspired
      the line "the rocket's red glare" in what later became "The Star-Spangled Banner."
    spriteName: congreve_rocket
  - id: goddard_nell
    displayName: Goddard "Nell"
    correctZoneId: 1926
    zoneLabel: 1926
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: The world's first liquid-propellant rocket, launched by Robert Goddard
      on March 16, 1926.
    triviaFact: That historic first flight lasted just 2.5 seconds, climbed only
      41 feet, and landed in a cabbage patch 184 feet away - a flight so modest it's
      still called "the liquid rocket's Kitty Hawk."
    spriteName: goddard_nell
  - id: v2_aggregat4
    displayName: V-2 (Aggregat-4)
    correctZoneId: 1942_1944
    zoneLabel: 1942 - 1944
    country: Germany
    keyAdvance: "The first long-range ballistic missile and the first human-made
      object to reach space, developed under Wernher von Braun at Peenem\xFCnde."
    triviaFact: After World War II, "Operation Paperclip" brought roughly 500 German
      rocket scientists and about 60 V-2s to the United States, while the Soviet
      Union took the production facilities and a similar number of rockets - seeding
      both space programs from the same source. A single V-2 stood about 14 m (46
      ft) tall, weighed roughly 12,500 kg (27,600 lb) fully fueled, and carried a
      1,000 kg (2,200 lb) warhead. Its single engine burned ethanol-water fuel with
      liquid oxygen to send the missile on a largely unguided ballistic arc reaching
      about four times the speed of sound over a range of roughly 320 km (200 mi).
    spriteName: v2_aggregat4
  - id: r7_sputnik
    displayName: R-7 / Sputnik Launcher
    correctZoneId: 1957
    zoneLabel: 1957
    country: Soviet Union (USSR)
    keyAdvance: The first orbital launch vehicle in history, launching Sputnik 1
      on October 4, 1957.
    triviaFact: The R-7 is the longest-serving rocket family ever built - a month
      after Sputnik 1, an R-7 launched Sputnik 2 carrying the dog Laika, the first
      living creature to reach orbit. Its core is flanked by four strap-on boosters,
      each driven by a four-chamber RD-107 engine, clustered around a central four-chamber
      RD-108 engine - 20 combustion chambers in all, burning kerosene and liquid
      oxygen for a combined liftoff thrust of roughly 5,000 kN (1.1 million lbf)
      from a vehicle weighing about 280 tonnes fully fueled. Descendants of this
      same basic design, continuously refined for almost seven decades, still launch
      Soyuz crews to the International Space Station today.
    spriteName: r7_sputnik
  - id: explorer1_juno
    displayName: Explorer 1 / Juno I
    correctZoneId: 1958
    zoneLabel: 1958
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: 'America''s answer to Sputnik: the first US satellite in orbit, launched
      January 31, 1958 on a repurposed Redstone missile called Juno I.'
    triviaFact: Explorer 1 weighed just 30.66 pounds, but its onboard instruments
      made one of the great discoveries of the Space Age - the Van Allen radiation
      belts circling Earth.
    spriteName: explorer1_juno
  - id: x15
    displayName: X-15
    correctZoneId: 1959_1968
    zoneLabel: 1959 - 1968
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: A rocket-powered airplane that bridged the gap between atmospheric
      flight and spaceflight, flying to the edge of space and back under its own
      wings.
    triviaFact: Across 199 flights, the X-15 reached 4,520 mph and 67 miles altitude
      - and future Apollo 11 commander Neil Armstrong was one of its twelve pilots.
    spriteName: x15
  - id: mercury_redstone
    displayName: Mercury-Redstone
    correctZoneId: 1961
    zoneLabel: 1961
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: A modified Redstone ballistic missile adapted to launch NASA's one-person
      Mercury capsule, putting the first American into space on a suborbital arc.
    triviaFact: On May 5, 1961, Alan Shepard rode Freedom 7 on a 15-minute-22-second
      suborbital hop - just 346,944 newtons (78,000 lb) of thrust from an engine
      burning ethyl alcohol, water, and liquid oxygen - barely three weeks before
      President Kennedy committed America to landing on the Moon. Standing 25.4 m
      (83 ft) tall and weighing about 30,000 kg (66,000 lb) fully fueled, the Mercury-Redstone
      flew exactly two crewed missions - Shepard's Freedom 7 and Gus Grissom's Liberty
      Bell 7 that July - before NASA moved on to the more powerful Atlas rocket for
      the Mercury program's four later orbital flights.
    spriteName: mercury_redstone
  - id: gemini_titan
    displayName: Gemini-Titan II
    correctZoneId: 1965_1966
    zoneLabel: 1965 - 1966
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: A modified Air Force ICBM that launched NASA's two-person Gemini
      capsules, proving the rendezvous, docking, and spacewalk techniques Apollo
      would need to reach the Moon.
    triviaFact: Gemini 8's Neil Armstrong and David Scott achieved the first-ever
      docking in space in March 1966; Ed White made the first American spacewalk
      on Gemini 4 two months earlier; and Gemini 7 set the era's endurance record
      - 330 hours 35 minutes, nearly 14 days, 206 orbits. Adapted from an Air Force
      ICBM, the two-stage Titan II stood about 33 m (109 ft) tall, weighed around
      154,200 kg (340,000 lb) at liftoff, and produced roughly 2,090 kN (470,000
      lbf) of thrust from hypergolic engines burning Aerozine 50 fuel with nitrogen
      tetroxide - propellants that ignite on contact, making it simpler and more
      reliable to launch on short notice than earlier cryogenic designs. It carried
      ten crewed Gemini missions in all, from Gemini 3 through Gemini 12.
    spriteName: gemini_titan
  - id: saturn_v
    displayName: Saturn V
    correctZoneId: 1967_1969
    zoneLabel: 1967 - 1969
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: The most powerful rocket ever flown successfully at the time - 110.6
      m (363 ft) tall, generating over 33 million newtons (7.5 million lb) of first-stage
      thrust alone - built to send astronauts to the Moon.
    triviaFact: Saturn V was taller than the Statue of Liberty and generated the
      thrust of 85 Hoover Dams at liftoff; its three stages produced 7.5 million,
      1 million, and 200,000 pounds of thrust in turn - and a modified third stage
      later launched Skylab, America's first space station, in 1973. Each of the
      first stage's five F-1 engines produced about 1.5 million pounds-force of thrust
      burning RP-1 kerosene with liquid oxygen, while the upper stages switched to
      J-2 engines burning liquid hydrogen for a sharper 421-second specific impulse
      in vacuum. NASA rated the fully stacked vehicle to place up to 311,000 lb (141,000
      kg) into low Earth orbit, and its last three Moon-bound flights each carried
      about 96,000 lb (43,500 kg) on toward the Moon.
    spriteName: saturn_v
  - id: space_shuttle
    displayName: Space Shuttle (STS)
    correctZoneId: 1981
    zoneLabel: 1981
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: The first reusable orbital spacecraft - a 4-part stack of a reusable
      Orbiter, two recoverable Solid Rocket Boosters, and one expendable External
      Tank - first launched April 12, 1981.
    triviaFact: STS-1's crew of John Young and Robert Crippen were the first ever
      to ride a brand-new space vehicle on its maiden flight. Its 3 main engines
      and twin SRBs made 7.3 million pounds of combined thrust at liftoff - and over
      135 missions, the 5-orbiter fleet carried more astronauts than every other
      rocket in history combined. Each Solid Rocket Booster carried about 1.1 million
      pounds of solid propellant and together supplied roughly 80% of the Shuttle's
      thrust for its first two minutes of flight, before burning out and parachuting
      into the Atlantic for reuse. The three Space Shuttle Main Engines, throttleable
      from 65% up to 109% of rated power, drew from an External Tank holding 526,000
      gallons of liquid hydrogen and liquid oxygen - consumed at roughly 62,300 gallons
      a minute during the climb to orbit.
    spriteName: space_shuttle
  - id: delta_iv_heavy
    displayName: Delta IV Heavy
    correctZoneId: 2004
    zoneLabel: 2004
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: One of the most powerful rockets of its generation, built by strapping
      three Delta IV boosters side by side to triple the available thrust.
    triviaFact: Delta IV Heavy's hydrogen-fueled engines ignite in a deliberate fireball
      at the base of the rocket moments before liftoff - a dramatic, expected sight
      that's become the rocket's visual signature. Standing 70.7 m (232 ft) tall
      and weighing about 733,000 kg (1.6 million lb) at liftoff, its three Common
      Booster Cores each carry a single RS-68 engine, together producing roughly
      9,420 kN (2.1 million lbf) of combined thrust - enough to lift about 28,370
      kg (62,550 lb) to low Earth orbit. Its final flight launched a classified US
      national security payload in April 2024, retiring the rocket after two decades
      of service.
    spriteName: delta_iv_heavy
  - id: falcon_9
    displayName: Falcon 9
    correctZoneId: 2015_2017
    zoneLabel: 2015 - 2017
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: The first orbital-class rocket booster to land and be reused.
    triviaFact: Falcon 9 achieved the first successful vertical landing of an orbital
      booster in December 2015 and the first re-flight of a used booster in March
      2017 - and its Dragon spacecraft became the first privately built and recovered
      orbital spacecraft in history. Standing 70 m (230 ft) tall and 3.7 m (12 ft)
      wide, its first stage's nine Merlin engines together produce about 1.7 million
      pounds-force of thrust at liftoff burning RP-1 kerosene with liquid oxygen,
      while four grid fins and deployable legs let the booster fly itself back down
      tail-first for reuse. A fully expendable Falcon 9 can deliver roughly 22,800
      kg (50,265 lb) to low Earth orbit, per SpaceX's own published specifications.
    spriteName: falcon_9
  - id: new_shepard
    displayName: New Shepard
    correctZoneId: 2021
    zoneLabel: 2021
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: A fully reusable suborbital rocket built specifically to carry paying
      passengers just past the edge of space and back.
    triviaFact: Named after Alan Shepard, the first American in space, New Shepard
      has flown more than a dozen crews on short suborbital hops that give passengers
      several minutes of weightlessness before parachuting back to the Texas desert.
      Its single BE-3 engine burns liquid hydrogen and liquid oxygen and can throttle
      down to about 18% power, producing up to 490 kN (110,000 lbf) of thrust - precise
      enough to land the booster itself tail-first back at the launch site for reuse.
      Its very first test flight in April 2015 reached roughly 93,500 m (307,000
      ft), just past the Karman line widely used to mark the edge of space.
    spriteName: new_shepard
  - id: sls
    displayName: SLS (Space Launch System)
    correctZoneId: 2022
    zoneLabel: 2022
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: NASA's super heavy-lift rocket for the Artemis program, designed
      to send astronauts and large cargo back to the Moon.
    triviaFact: At 322 feet tall, SLS is taller than the Statue of Liberty and produces
      15% more thrust than the Saturn V - its first flight, Artemis I, sent an uncrewed
      Orion capsule 280,000 miles from Earth, farther than any spacecraft built for
      humans had ever traveled. Its core stage is powered by four RS-25 engines -
      the same engine design that powered the Space Shuttle for over three decades
      across 135 missions - here throttled up to 109% of their original rated thrust
      (111% on later flights) for more than two million pounds-force of combined
      vacuum thrust. NASA's first three SLS flights even reuse genuine veteran engines
      flown on past Shuttle missions before newly built RS-25s take over.
    spriteName: sls
  - id: starship
    displayName: Starship
    correctZoneId: 2023
    zoneLabel: 2023
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: A fully reusable super-heavy lift vehicle designed for Moon and Mars
      missions.
    triviaFact: Starship is the largest and most powerful rocket ever built, and
      its April 2023 flight was the first integrated test of the full two-stage vehicle.
      Its Super Heavy booster stands about 71 m (233 ft) tall and 9 m (30 ft) wide,
      with 33 Raptor engines burning methane and liquid oxygen - a "methalox" mix
      chosen partly because methane can be manufactured on Mars - to produce more
      than 73 meganewtons (16.5 million pounds-force) of thrust at full power, easily
      surpassing the Saturn V's. The booster alone carries about 3,400 tonnes of
      propellant, roughly 2,700 tonnes of liquid oxygen and 700 tonnes of methane.
    spriteName: starship
  rocketSprites:
